course: dictionary
lesson: 3955
title: Furious: very, very angry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Furious» is a strong word for anger. Being a little cross is not the same as being furious. Use the clues around it: «He felt lied to and furious.» Being lied to makes someone angry, and the word furious shows how strong that anger is.
explain: The word can also describe things that are very strong, fast or wild. «A furious storm» is a storm with huge winds and rain, and «at a furious speed» means very, very fast. Look at the words next to furious to decide which meaning fits.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «He's shocked and furious, and storms home.» Could you put very, very angry in place of furious? Yes, so it fits. Storms home shows you what that anger looks like in the story.
fact: «Furious» means very, very angry.
fact: «Furious» can also mean very strong, fast or wild, as in «a furious storm» or «at a furious speed».
fact: You can be furious with someone, or furious about something: «furious with someone», «furious about something».
fact: In Wales, «tamping» means furious, and «cwtch» means a cuddle.
fact: In a story, the White Rabbit worries the Duchess will be «savage» (furious) if he keeps her waiting.
exercise: «He felt lied to and furious.» What does furious mean here? | *Very, very angry | Mildly surprised | Tired and hungry | Very quiet
exercise: «A furious storm» rolled in over the sea. What does furious describe the storm as? | Calm and still | *Very strong and wild | Warm and sunny | Slow and gentle
exercise: Which phrase uses furious to mean very fast? | furious with the teacher | furious about the noise | furious and sad | *at a furious pace
test: Tom says «furious» is just a fancy word for cross. Why is that not quite right? | *Furious is stronger than cross, meaning very, very angry | Furious means happy | Furious means tired | Furious and cross mean exactly the same thing, so there is no difference
test: In the story, the Rabbit worries the Duchess will be «savage» if he is late. What does savage mean here? | *Furious | Sleepy | Gentle and kind | Lost
exam: Which sentence uses furious to mean very strong or fast? | He was furious when he lost the game | She felt furious about the broken toy | The dog was furious with the postman | *The horses galloped at a furious speed
